MON syntax is implemented for supplied arguments and compile-time anonymous
const records. Runtime anonymous records remain deferred. The MON format contract
is accepted, with its Rust-only `moth::mon` codec implemented and Moth-native integration deferred.

MON means Moth Object Notation. MON syntax gives supplied arguments and record
values a common notation. Pipes declare fields or parameters. Parentheses supply
values or group an expression.

[codeblock, $code("moth"):
    Size = |
        width Int,
        height Int,
    |

    size = Size(width = 1280, height = 720)

    settings #= (
        title = "Strategy",
        window = (width = 1280, height = 720),
    )
]

Every anonymous field has a name and a value. Records can nest directly.
`#=` requires all their values to be known during compilation.

`(value)` groups an expression. `(name = value)` constructs a one-field record.
Commas separate entries, and a final comma is optional. Moth has no tuple values.

Named types still need their constructors. An anonymous record with matching
fields does not become a `Size`.

Moth MON source syntax allows ordinary expressions according to the receiving
Moth context. MON serialised data is self-contained literal data only, and a MON
reader never evaluates Moth expressions, including foldable ones.

@../mon/#mon-format (MON format) owns the serialised literal-data contract.
This page owns the shared notation only.

The Rust-only `moth::mon` codec is implemented. Moth-native `$mon`,
automatic schema extraction and the static project builder remain deferred
separately. This syntax alone does not turn a record into text or make every
Moth value serialisable.
